And so on. That has been elaborated by us. Likewise, 'su-mama' — 'well, not existing as mine' — of him for whom, because of being established in the fullness of 'I'-consciousness, the universe is not anything that could be designated separately with the force of the sixty meanings [of the genitive case]. For those initiated in other scriptures — the <s>vijñānākalas</s> and <s>pralayakevalins</s> — even though there is nothing separate as 'mine,' nevertheless whatever latent tendency toward eligibility for difference might exist would expand at the time of awakening, preventing the ascent to 'I'-consciousness. To eliminate that, the word 'su' in the sense of 'well/completely.' As I have said in my hymn:
Quote: 'That nothing whatsoever is mine — by this, inert creatures perpetually attain wretchedness;
that nothing whatsoever is mine, I am everything — having shouldered this exalted burden, I stand.'

And so on. And there again, following the <s>Bhinnayoni Mālinī Bhaṭṭārikā</s>, even for the letters beginning with 'pha' which have obtained placement in other <s>tattvas</s> through the arrangement of the <s>Abhinnayoni Mātṛkā</s>,
